@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+## Name
+
+mnist_robustness
+
+## Title
+
+Lightweight Data Augmentation for Robust Digit Classification
+
+## Short Hypothesis
+
+A small CNN trained with simple augmentation, regularization, and tuned optimizer settings will improve robustness across MNIST, Fashion-MNIST, and KMNIST.
+
+## Related Work
+
+Prior work shows that data augmentation and regularization often improve generalization, but the effect size can vary across datasets and corruption types. We focus on a minimal, reproducible setup that still tests transfer across multiple HuggingFace datasets.
+
+## Abstract
+
+We study whether a lightweight regularization recipe can improve the robustness of a small convolutional network on digit-like image datasets. The method combines simple data augmentation, weight decay, dropout, momentum tuning, and early stopping. We evaluate on MNIST plus two additional HuggingFace datasets, Fashion-MNIST and KMNIST, to compare clean accuracy and robustness under rotation and Gaussian noise shifts. The goal is to produce a small but clear experiment that demonstrates how basic training choices affect robustness across closely related vision benchmarks.
+
+## Experiments
+
+- Train a small CNN baseline on MNIST with standard preprocessing.
+- Add random rotation and affine augmentation, then compare test accuracy on clean and shifted data.
+- Tune learning rate, batch size, momentum, dropout, and weight decay on MNIST before transferring the best settings to Fashion-MNIST and KMNIST.
+- Report accuracy, loss curves, and error examples on rotated and noisy test sets for all three datasets.
+- Perform a short ablation over augmentation strength, momentum, dropout, and weight decay values.
+
+## Risk Factors And Limitations
+
+- The digit datasets are still relatively simple, so gains may be modest.
+- The shifted test sets may not fully capture real-world robustness.
+- Very small models may underfit if regularization is too strong.
